09-02 13:44:48.641 10071 10091 I ParitySuite: {"model":"SmolLM3-3B-INT8-INT4","engine":"ExecuTorchEngine","params":"greedy topK=1 seed=7 maxTokens=768","cases":[{"id":"trap-arithmetic","status":"pass","content":"9","reasoning_chars":1845,"raw":"<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure this out. The problem says a farmer has 17 sheep. Then it says \"All but 9 run away.\" Hmm, I need to understand what that means.\n\nFirst, the total number of sheep is 17. The phrase \"All but 9 run away\" – that probably means that the majority of the sheep ran away, leaving only some behind. So if all but 9 stayed, then the number of sheep that stayed would be 9, right? Because if 9 didn't run away, then the rest (17 - 9) did. \n\nWait, let me make sure I'm interpreting this correctly. Sometimes these riddles can be tricky with wording. The key is \"All but 9 run away.\" So if \"All but X\" means that X remained, then yes, X is the number that didn't run away. So if \"All but 9\" (i.e., 9) didn't run away, then the farmer has 9 sheep left. \n\nAlternatively, if \"All but 9 run away,\" that could mean that the sheep that didn't run away are the ones that \"All but 9\" implies. Wait, that might not make sense. Let me think again. \n\nThe structure of the sentence is \"All but X run away,\" so the number of sheep that ran away is (total - X). So if the total is 17, and X is 9, then the number that ran away is 17 - 9 = 8. Therefore, the number that remained is 9. \n\nSo the farmer has 9 sheep left. That seems straightforward. But let me check with an example. Suppose the farmer had, say, 10 sheep. If \"All but 3 run away,\" then 10 - 3 = 7 ran away, so 3 stayed. So applying that to this problem, 17 - 9 = 8 ran away, leaving 9. \n\nI think that's correct. The answer should be 9. Let me just confirm once more. The problem is phrased as \"All but 9 run away,\" so the ones that didn't run away are the 9. Therefore, the farmer has 9 sheep left. Yeah, that makes sense. I don't see any ambiguity here. The key is understanding that \"All but X\" refers to the number that remained, not the number that ran away. So the answer is 9.\n<\/think>\n\n9","calls":[],"prompt_tokens":277,"cached_tokens":0,"generated_tokens":516,"prefill_ms":12885,"decode_ms":40078},{"id":"multi-step-change","status":"pass","content":"Ana buys 7 pencils at ₱12 each, totaling ₱84 (7 × 12 = 84). She pays with ₱100, so the change is ₱100 − ₱84 = ₱16.\n\n\\boxed{16}","reasoning_chars":709,"raw":"<think>\nOkay, let's see. Ana bought 7 pencils, each costing 12 pesos. First, I need to calculate the total cost of the pencils. So, 7 pencils times 12 pesos each. Let me do that multiplication. 7 times 12 is 84. So the total cost is 84 pesos.\n\nNow, she paid with a 100-peso bill. To find out how much change she should receive, I need to subtract the total cost from the amount she paid. That would be 100 pesos minus 84 pesos. Let me calculate that. 100 minus 80 is 20, and then minus 4 more is 16. So the change should be 16 pesos. Wait, that seems straightforward. Let me double-check the math to be sure. 7 times 12 is indeed 84. 100 minus 84 is 16. Yep, that's correct. So Ana should get 16 pesos back as change.\n<\/think>\n\nAna buys 7 pencils at ₱12 each, totaling ₱84 (7 × 12 = 84). She pays with ₱100, so the change is ₱100 − ₱84 = ₱16.\n\n\\boxed{16}","calls":[],"prompt_tokens":283,"cached_tokens":0,"generated_tokens":253,"prefill_ms":13859,"decode_ms":18274},{"id":"format-constraint","status":"pass","content":"<think>\nOkay, let me see. The user wants a
